# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/003_volatile_side_effect_preservation.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_volatile_side_effect_preservation
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = A volatile access trace that must survive every optimization level
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Each counter is a real volatile object, so every access is part of the program's observable
behaviour and may not be elided or reordered by the optimizer.  Each is written and then
read back, and every value read is printed immediately, so the trace records the individual
accesses rather than only a final result.  All arithmetic stays inside the range of int.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
counter_initial=0
after_step1 returned=1 counter=1
after_step2 returned=2 counter=2
after_step3 returned=3 counter=3
sink_a=11
sink_b=22
sink_c=33
loop_step=0 counter=4
loop_step=1 counter=5
loop_step=2 counter=6
loop_step=3 counter=7
loop_step=4 counter=8
counter_final=8
END
